WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.580
Which population is in more danger?

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.120
One shrinks a little on average each year.

3
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.300
The other grows a little, but its numbers swing wildly.

4
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.180
Both start with fifty animals.

5
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:19.273
Put one population on a chart.

6
00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:23.473
Each blue line is one possible run of its numbers.

7
00:00:24.773 --> 00:00:28.793
The red dashed line is the floor, a number too low to recover from.

8
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.267
Two things move the line.

9
00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:36.647
The gold arrow is the trend, the average yearly change on a log scale.

10
00:00:38.207 --> 00:00:40.027
Around it the line jitters up and down.

11
00:00:41.287 --> 00:00:43.647
Those are the swings, the good years and bad.

12
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:47.627
Now run the same population two hundred times.

13
00:00:48.907 --> 00:00:52.207
The runs fan out. Some touch the floor.

14
00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:55.567
The fraction that touches it is the risk.

15
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:03.280
That is the idea behind a population viability analysis A real one runs a fuller

16
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.340
model many times, and counts the runs that fall.

17
00:01:07.383 --> 00:01:09.623
Turn two dials, one at a time.

18
00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:13.383
Widen the swings, and more runs touch the floor.

19
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:16.663
Raise the start, and fewer runs do.

20
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:21.907
Now tilt the gold arrow down, so the population shrinks on average.

21
00:01:23.227 --> 00:01:26.207
Will starting with ten times as many animals save it?

22
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.500
Over one century, the bigger start helps a lot.

23
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.360
Far fewer runs touch the floor.

24
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:40.820
But keep the clock running.

25
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.200
Over a thousand years, nearly every run reaches it.

26
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:49.103
The bigger population bought time, not safety.

27
00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:54.243
With the arrow pointing down, every start reaches the floor in the end.

28
00:01:55.463 --> 00:01:58.983
How soon depends on the trend, the start and the swings together.

29
00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:03.363
Tilt it up to plus point oh two, and most runs stay clear.

30
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:08.217
With few animals, one unlucky litter can decide the outcome.

31
00:02:09.497 --> 00:02:13.157
So chance in single births and deaths weighs most near the floor.

32
00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:18.927
Genes can tilt the arrow too.

33
00:02:20.327 --> 00:02:22.907
Fewer animals means more mating among relatives.

34
00:02:24.327 --> 00:02:29.227
Inbreeding can lower survival and fertility, so the trend tilts down and numbers

35
00:02:29.227 --> 00:02:33.247
fall further. This loop is called the extinction vortex.

36
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.090
Back to the two populations.

37
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.910
The shrinking one will reach the floor in time, however big it starts.

38
00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.350
The growing one can escape.

39
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.050
Its risk still depends on trend, size and swings together.

40
00:02:49.183 --> 00:02:53.323
This gives managers a target, for one population in one habitat.

41
00:02:54.643 --> 00:02:59.663
A minimum viable population is the smallest start that keeps the risk below a chosen

42
00:02:59.663 --> 00:03:01.743
level, over a chosen time.

43
00:03:02.817 --> 00:03:06.357
So conservation starts by removing the cause of decline.

44
00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:12.617
Alongside it, numbers, connections and genes can lift the trend or cut the risk.

45
00:03:13.757 --> 00:03:17.777
But in this model, without a trend above zero, size only buys time.
